      <memberdef kind="function" id="classStore_1aaf81da54b109478891030b7e04653bad" prot="public" static="yes" nodiscard="yes" const="no" explicit="no" inline="yes" virt="non-virtual">
        <templateparamlist>
          <param>
            <type>typename T</type>
          </param>
        </templateparamlist>
        <type>std::shared_ptr&lt; T &gt;</type>
        <definition>static std::shared_ptr&lt; T &gt; Store::cachedObject</definition>
        <argsstring>()</argsstring>
        <name>cachedObject</name>
        <qualifiedname>Store::cachedObject</qualifiedname>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Thread-local cached lookup of T, refreshed only when the <ref refid="classStore" kindref="compound">Store</ref> generation changes. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Each thread keeps its own shared_ptr and the generation value it last saw. The generation is loaded with acquire semantics, pairing with the release fetch_add in <ref refid="classStore_1a2a4bfc0b18f2999216eda3067988cf57" kindref="member">replaceObject()</ref>, so a thread that observes a new generation also observes the replacement. A mismatch or an empty cache triggers a full <ref refid="classStore_1a0607fb3b583c06ca910ed6c9881e790c" kindref="member">getObject&lt;T&gt;()</ref> lookup; otherwise the cached pointer is returned without touching the mutex. <verbatim>     In plain words: looking in the registry needs a lock, which is
</verbatim> too slow to do for every web request. So each thread remembers the pointer it got last time. &quot;Acquire&quot; and &quot;release&quot; are a pair of promises: after <ref refid="classStore_1a2a4bfc0b18f2999216eda3067988cf57" kindref="member">replaceObject()</ref> publishes a new pointer with &quot;release&quot;, any thread that reads the new number with &quot;acquire&quot; is also guaranteed to see that new pointer, not a stale one. This is cheap because a normal read of the counter is enough most of the time; the lock is only used when something actually changed. <parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>T</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Type to look up; on first use it must be constructible by <ref refid="classStore_1a0607fb3b583c06ca910ed6c9881e790c" kindref="member">getObject&lt;T&gt;()</ref> with no arguments. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>A shared_ptr to the current singleton for T. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>The returned pointer is nodiscard; callers should use it instead of re-fetching through <ref refid="classStore_1a0607fb3b583c06ca910ed6c9881e790c" kindref="member">getObject()</ref>. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="classStore_1a0607fb3b583c06ca910ed6c9881e790c" kindref="member">getObject()</ref>, <ref refid="classStore_1a2a4bfc0b18f2999216eda3067988cf57" kindref="member">replaceObject()</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
