    <briefdescription>
<para>Process-wide service locator that maps a type to one shared instance. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The class has no non-static state; the actual registry is a set of file-scope static members defined in <ref refid="store_8cpp" kindref="compound">store.cpp</ref>. Access is guarded by a single mutex, and a separate atomic generation counter lets per-thread caches notice replacements. Objects are owned through shared_ptr&lt;void&gt; and recovered by static_pointer_cast using typeid(T) as the key, so a lookup by type resolves to the concrete pointer stored for that type. <verbatim>     In plain words: a &quot;singleton&quot; is one single object shared by the
</verbatim> entire program. You never create a <ref refid="classStore" kindref="compound">Store</ref> object; you just call its static functions. A shared_ptr is a smart pointer that keeps its object alive for as long as anyone still holds it, then destroys it automatically when the last holder lets go, so no manual delete is needed. typeid(T) is a small tag that identifies a C++ type while the program runs, which lets the map be keyed by type instead of by a name. A mutex is a lock that only one thread may hold at a time, which stops two threads from changing the map at once and corrupting it. static_pointer_cast simply relabels the generic stored pointer as a T pointer; it performs no runtime type check. <simplesect kind="note"><para>registerObject refuses duplicates; getObject lazily constructs on first use; replaceObject overwrites without throwing and is intended for injecting test doubles. (&quot;Lazily&quot; means the object is not built until somebody actually asks for it; a &quot;test double&quot; is a fake object used in place of the real one during tests.) </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para>tests/support/fake_db.hpp </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
